// @reject: decision 20 — modifying requires a destination with a NAME. As a
// @reject: statement, `f()[1]$~ 5` would modify a temporary nobody holds: the
// @reject: array the call returned is discarded on the next line, so the edit is
// @reject: unobservable by construction.
// @reject: The two Rust engines already refuse it while parsing; the browser
// @reject: engine runs it and says nothing, which is the silent no-op again.
f() { <~ [1, 2, 3] }
f()[1]$~ 5
